<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johan Risingh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1617">1617</a>–<a href="/years/1672">1672</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johan Risingh was a Swedish official and the last governor of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, from 1654 to 1655. On arriving he seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-casimir">Fort Casimir</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delaware-river">Delaware River</a> and renamed it Fort Trinity. In response <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-stuyvesant">Peter Stuyvesant</a> led a Dutch force from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-amsterdam">New Amsterdam</a> in 1655 that captured the Swedish forts and ended New Sweden. Risingh also wrote one of the first Swedish works on economics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johan-risingh.jpg" alt="Johan Risingh" width="210" height="123" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johan Classon Risingh · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signature_of_Johan_Classon_Risingh_(1617%E2%80%931672).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
